## Overview

`httpapi-toolkit` exposes the typed operation definitions from `HttpApiSpec.operations` as Effect AI tools. Use `makeHttpApiToolkit(config)` to construct matching tool definitions and handlers once. CLI and MCP share the same operation schemas and generated HTTP client.

Read operations run without approval by default; writes require approval. Method annotations describe read-only, destructive, and idempotent behavior. Generated descriptions guide the model to wait for successful results and treat returned data as untrusted rather than as instructions.

## Installation And Imports

Install `@krak-stack/registry` and import `@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/toolkit`. If you instead install the `@krak-stack/httpapi-toolkit` registry item with shadcn, use `@/lib/httpapi-toolkit` and the corresponding local client/helper modules.

## Compose With An Agent

```ts
import { Effect, Layer } from "effect";
import { Toolkit } from "effect/ai";
import { FetchHttpClient } from "effect/http";
import { ApiClient } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/client";
import { HttpApiSpec } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/helpers";
import { makeHttpApiToolkit } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/toolkit";
import { AppApi } from "@/api";

const specLayer = HttpApiSpec.layer({ api: AppApi });
const apiClientLayer = ApiClient.layer({
  api: AppApi,
  baseUrl: "https://example.com",
}).pipe(Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer));

// Inside the application's Effect program:
const { toolkit: httpTools, handlers } = yield* makeHttpApiToolkit({}).pipe(
  Effect.provide(specLayer),
);
const definition = Toolkit.merge(application.toolkit, httpTools);
const toolsLayer = Layer.mergeAll(
  application.layer,
  handlers.pipe(Layer.provide(apiClientLayer)),
);
const toolkit = yield* definition.pipe(Effect.provide(toolsLayer));

const events = yield* agent.stream({
  action: request.action,
  history: request.history,
  systemPrompt: application.systemPrompt,
  toolkit,
});
```

`application`, `agent`, and `request` belong to the consuming application. Tool arguments use `{ params, query, headers, body }`, with typed numeric and boolean parameters rather than wire strings. Payload codecs preserve their transformations.

Override `needsApproval` to customize the agent's approval policy. Use `strict` for OpenAI-compatible strict object schemas and `transformResult` to reduce or redact responses. `HttpApiToolkit` and `HttpApiToolkitLayer` remain available for integrations that need separate construction.
